{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And a man found him, and behold, he was wandering in the field.</b> This is Gabriel, for since it says 'and a man found him,' infer from this that this man was seeking Joseph and searching after him, and he found him, but Joseph did not find him. And his intention was to warn him that he should be on guard against his brothers, for he saw him walking in the field, wandering in his mind — for he went in peace, yet there is no peace for his brothers with him. And if so, this is certainly an angel, for he knew what was in his heart; for otherwise, how could any other man know that he was walking as one astray — perhaps this is simply his way, for without doubt he was walking on some road or path." "And its midrashic sense: he went astray regarding the matter of the field mentioned in the story of Cain and Abel (Genesis 4:8), for Joseph should have taken to heart what happened to Abel with Cain — that from the side of jealousy a man kills his brother. And Joseph reasoned: granted, Cain, who killed his brother — that was because it says 'and it came to pass when they were in the field' (Genesis 4:8) — on account of a matter of a field, as he said: 'the field over which you are standing is mine.' If so, there was a cause for the matter. But my brothers — why should they kill me for nothing, for jealousy over a striped tunic is not comparable to jealousy over a field. This is what is written: 'and behold, he was wandering in the field' (Genesis 37:15) — for this field mentioned in connection with Cain led him astray, and he did not know that the nature of jealousy demands that over a trifling matter a man will rise against his fellow and murder him."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:32:21.635122275Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}